16	/// you can read, review in a pull request and roll back is the whole reason to be on EF at all.</para>
17	///
18	/// <para><b>The id is pinned.</b> <c>00000000000001_InitialCreate</c> rather than a timestamp,
19	/// because a timestamp would make two builds of the same definition produce different applications —
20	/// and the second build would then look like a schema change to anyone comparing them. Every
21	/// migration the user adds afterwards gets a real timestamp from EF, which sorts after this one.</para>
22	///
23	/// <para><b>The snapshot is what makes the next migration possible.</b> EF diffs a new model against
24	/// the snapshot, so a snapshot that does not exactly describe this schema makes the user's first
25	/// <c>dotnet ef migrations add</c> emit changes nobody asked for. That the two agree is not
26	/// established by care: <c>MigrationFidelityTests</c> generates an application, builds it, runs
27	/// <c>dotnet ef migrations add</c> and asserts the result is EMPTY.</para>
28	/// </summary>
